rcutils_strndup: bound memchr's length by PTRDIFF_MAX.

rcutils_strdup() calls rcutils_strndup(str, SIZE_MAX), which calls
memchr(str, '\0', SIZE_MAX). QNX 8's x86_64 memchr compares the length
signed (cmovle): SIZE_MAX is -1 there, it indexes its alignment jump table
at -1 and jumps to address 0. librcl's load-time constructor makes exactly
that call when RMW_IMPLEMENTATION is set, so every ROS 2 node died before
main() with SIGSEGV. No object is larger than PTRDIFF_MAX, so the bound
changes nothing on any other system.

Upstream-Status: Pending (a QNX libc defect worked around)

--- a/src/strdup.c
+++ b/src/strdup.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 
 #include <limits.h>
 #include <stddef.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
 #include <string.h>
 
 #include "./common.h"
@@ -44,7 +45,11 @@
     return NULL;
   }
   RCUTILS_CHECK_ALLOCATOR(&allocator, return NULL);
-  char * p = memchr(str, '\0', max_length);
+  // QNX 8's x86_64 memchr compares the length as a signed value: with
+  // SIZE_MAX (what rcutils_strdup passes) it indexes its jump table at -1 and
+  // jumps to address 0. No object is larger than PTRDIFF_MAX, so this bound
+  // changes nothing anywhere else.
+  char * p = memchr(str, '\0', max_length > PTRDIFF_MAX ? PTRDIFF_MAX : max_length);
   size_t string_length = p == NULL ? max_length : (size_t)(p - str);
   char * new_string = allocator.allocate(string_length + 1, allocator.state);
   if (NULL == new_string) {
